"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[4921],{4921:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"03-otkladyvat","title":"Lesson 03. Pay yourself first: How to save when nothing seems available","sourceFingerprint":"d6214d4b6f0ec5441eeebf96c56bf803b94579f366632f1e65eda68c368eea90","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"## Pay yourself first"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"How to save when nothing seems available"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"After the audit you have figures: how much you spend and where it leaks. Now turn them into money that accumulates. At this stage most people make the same mistake."},{"id":"b004","markdown":"### Why “I will save whatever remains” fails"},{"id":"b005","markdown":"The familiar pattern: salary arrives and you live for a month. At the end, you save whatever remains. Almost always nothing remains. This is not about willpower; it is how spending works. Money on a card feels available. Available money always finds somewhere to go: a birthday, a sale, a broken kettle or simply a difficult week."},{"id":"b006","markdown":"Saving at month-end means accumulating what you did not manage to spend. Saving at the start means spending what you did not save. It sounds like wordplay, but in practice it is the difference between zero and savings."},{"id":"b007","markdown":"### Pay yourself first"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"Reverse the pattern. Money arrives and a predetermined share goes to a separate account the same day. Live on the rest. Pay yourself first as a mandatory payment, like rent or communications. Within a couple of months you become used to the remainder, as though your original salary had been slightly lower."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"WHEN TO SAVE. “SAVE WHAT REMAINS”: SALARY, MONTHLY SPENDING, REMAINING: 0 RUB. PAY YOURSELF FIRST: SALARY, 8% TO YOURSELF THE SAME DAY, LIVE ON THE REST. LIKE A TAX YOU PAY YOURSELF."},{"id":"b010","markdown":"### A percentage or an amount"},{"id":"b011","markdown":"Consider personal income tax. Your employer withholds 13% from every salary before it reaches you. Over the years this becomes so normal you do not even count that money as yours. If you had to pay a fixed tax amount yourself monthly, it would hurt every time."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"Arrange savings similarly: a tax you pay yourself, as a percentage, before you see the money."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"A percentage has another advantage over a fixed amount. Remember lesson 1’s cupboards filling however much space exists? If you save a fixed 10 000 RUB, a pay rise can flow entirely into spending. A percentage grows automatically with income, without a separate decision."},{"id":"b014","markdown":"If a month is weak, the amount automatically becomes smaller, so saving does not become an unbearable obligation you want to abandon."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"### Which percentage to choose"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"Return to the audit and find average monthly leaks. Divide them by average monthly income to obtain a starting share. You were already wasting that money. For example, take-home pay of 85 000 RUB and leaks of 21 000 RUB over 3 months mean 7 000 RUB monthly. 7 000 / 85 000 ≈ 8%: start there."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"If there are almost no leaks, begin with 3–5%. Even 1% beats zero: the habit matters more than the amount initially. A future aim is 10–20% of income, but not from the first month."},{"id":"b018","markdown":"### How to increase the share without pain"},{"id":"b019","markdown":"In 2004, economists Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi published the Save More Tomorrow experiment. Employees were asked not to save more immediately, but to agree in advance that their saving share would increase with every future salary raise."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"Over 40 months, participants’ average saving share rose from 3.5% to 13.6%. They did not have to cut their current life; they allocated part of money they had not yet seen. Do likewise: with every raise or bonus, increase saving by 1–2 percentage points and freely spend the rest. Within a couple of years you may reach roughly 15% almost without noticing."},{"id":"b021","markdown":"What share should you save, and how can you increase it without pain?"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"Monthly take-home income: 85 000 RUB. Leaks over 3 months from the audit: 21 000 RUB. Share: 8%."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"Starting share: 7 000 RUB monthly leaks \xf7 85 000 RUB."},{"id":"b024","markdown":"Future raises: 0. With each raise:"},{"id":"b025","markdown":"+1 percentage point or +2 percentage points. You save 6 800 RUB monthly. This is money that previously leaked away."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"### Automation"},{"id":"b027","markdown":"Willpower runs out; automatic payments do not."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"Most people leave defaults unchanged for years: mobile plans, paid bank services and phone settings. Money works similarly. In 2001 economists Brigitte Madrian and Dennis Shea studied an American company that automatically enrolled new employees into a retirement plan with an opt-out. Participation among newcomers rose from 37% to 86%, although essentially only the default checkbox changed."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"Make saving the default, and spending saved money require effort. Setting this up takes around 15 minutes."},{"id":"b030","markdown":"- Open a savings account, preferably at another bank rather than the salary bank. The harder it is to access, the less temptation. Lessons 6 and 9 cover account selection."},{"id":"b031","markdown":"- Set an automatic transfer for payday or the following day. Apps usually call it “automatic payment” or “recurring transfer”. Transfers through Russia’s Faster Payments System between your accounts at different banks are free within 30 million RUB monthly."},{"id":"b032","markdown":"- Remove the account from the app’s home screen. Not seeing it reduces the temptation to spend it."},{"id":"b033","markdown":"Important: if salary arrives in 2 parts, arrange transfers on both dates, otherwise only half will accumulate."},{"id":"b034","markdown":"### If income is irregular"},{"id":"b035","markdown":"Freelancers, self-employed people and those whose income depends heavily on bonuses need a slightly different approach."},{"id":"b036","markdown":"- Split every receipt immediately: a saving share, a tax share if self-employed or a sole trader, and the remainder for living."},{"id":"b037","markdown":"- Calculate a “minimum month”: mandatory spending from basket 1."},{"id":"b038","markdown":"- Live roughly on that minimum month. In good months, reserve the excess; cover weak months from that reserve."},{"id":"b039","markdown":"Income starts behaving almost like a salary even if receipts fluctuate."},{"id":"b040","markdown":"### Different goals, different baskets"},{"id":"b041","markdown":"Keeping all savings in one pile is inconvenient. Separate them by purpose:"},{"id":"b042","markdown":"- An emergency cushion against loss of income, untouched without serious reason; lesson 6."},{"id":"b043","markdown":"- Goals over 1–3 years: a holiday, renovation or down payment."},{"id":"b044","markdown":"- Long-term capital that will eventually work for you for years. This is the next module’s subject."},{"id":"b045","markdown":"Many banks allow several savings accounts or named savings pots, making each goal visible immediately."},{"id":"b046","markdown":"### What if there are loans"},{"id":"b047","markdown":"You still need to set money aside. The destination depends on rates. A credit card at 50% a year consumes more than a deposit at 13% earns. Every ruble repaid therefore saves you 50%. But paying every last penny towards debt is dangerous: the first breakdown sends you back into borrowing."},{"id":"b048","markdown":"First build a small reserve covering 1 month of expenses. Then direct all money set aside towards expensive debt. Details in lesson 5."},{"id":"b049","markdown":"### The essentials"},{"id":"b050","markdown":"- Save on the day money arrives, not at month-end."},{"id":"b051","markdown":"- Save a percentage. Start with the share represented by leaks or 3–5%."},{"id":"b052","markdown":"- With each income increase, raise the saving share by 1–2 percentage points."},{"id":"b053","markdown":"- A separate account at another bank and an automatic payday transfer do the work for you."},{"id":"b054","markdown":"Next lesson we examine debt: what is reasonable and what consumes you."}],"terms":[{"id":"ndfl","term":"Personal income tax (NDFL)","definition":"Personal income tax. This course’s educational calculations use 13% up to 2.4 million RUB of annual income and 15% for the next band. Since 2025 the main scale has been progressive; special rules apply to other types of income. Check current rates and deduction eligibility with the Federal Tax Service."},{"id":"pervonachalnyy-vznos","term":"Down payment","definition":"The portion of the property price you pay yourself when taking a mortgage. “Only 10%” sounds manageable, but the remaining mortgage can carry high interest for decades."},{"id":"protsentnyy-punkt","term":"Percentage point","definition":"The difference between two percentages. A rate rising from 4% to 5% is up 1 percentage point, but up 25% relative to the rate itself. They are constantly confused, and dramatic headlines exploit that confusion."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"03-otkladyvat-q1","q":"Why does saving “whatever remains at month-end” usually fail?","options":["Available money almost always finds somewhere to go, leaving zero at month-end","Banks prohibit transfers at month-end","Taxes are higher at month-end"],"correct":0},{"id":"03-otkladyvat-q2","q":"Why save a percentage of income rather than a fixed amount?","options":["A fixed amount is taxed","A percentage is always smaller than a fixed amount","A percentage adapts to unstable income and rises with salary increases"],"correct":2},{"id":"03-otkladyvat-q3","q":"Your salary rises by 10 000 RUB. What does Save More Tomorrow suggest?","options":["Spend the whole raise; you earned it","Save part of the raise by increasing the share by 1–2 percentage points, and spend the rest","Save the entire raise"],"correct":1}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);